---
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../SectionHead.astro";
import Link from "../Link.astro";
import EmbeddingPipeline from "./software/EmbeddingPipeline.astro";
import MorphospacePlot from "./biocosmos/MorphospacePlot.tsx";
import PlannerPlot from "./biocosmos/PlannerPlot.tsx";
import MimicryPlot from "./biocosmos/MimicryPlot.tsx";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import lepiverseHome from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-home.jpg";
import lepiverseSpecies from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-species.jpg";
import lepiverseSimilar from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-similar-species.jpg";
import mimicry from "../../data/biocosmos/mimicry.json";
import stats from "../../data/biocosmos/morphospace-stats.json";

const morphoStats = [
  {
    value: stats.species.toLocaleString(),
    label: "species in the morphospace",
  },
  {
    value: stats.bothSides.toLocaleString(),
    label: "seen from both wing sides",
  },
  {
    value: `${stats.generaSignificant}/${stats.genera}`,
    label:
      "genera with integrated dorsal and ventral patterns (Mantel p < 0.05)",
  },
  {
    value: `r = ${stats.scopeMantelR}`,
    label: "dorso-ventral integration across all species",
  },
];

const random = mimicry.permutations.find((p) => p.null === "Random")!;
const mimicryStats = [
  {
    value: `${mimicry.pairs.filter((p) => p.recovered).length}/${mimicry.counts.testedPairs}`,
    label:
      "pairs mutually in the top 10 of 8,409 species on the dorsal side",
  },
  {
    value: random.observed.toFixed(3),
    label: "mean dorsal partner percentile (chance = 0.50)",
  },
  {
    value: "p ≤ 0.0001",
    label: "against random and congener nulls (10,000 permutations)",
  },
];

// From the lepiverse.com collection summary (October 2026).
const collection = [
  { value: "619,739", label: "specimen images" },
  { value: "9,342", label: "species" },
  { value: "196", label: "countries" },
  { value: "8", label: "validated families" },
];

const shotClasses =
  "group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-forest-50 p-1.5 transition duration-500 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-900/40";
const figureClasses =
  "rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-white/70 p-3 shadow-sm dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-950/40 sm:p-5";
---

<SectionHead
  text="AI-assisted phenomics: quantifying complex traits at scale"
/>
<Content>
  <p>
    Coloration is a complex, multidimensional phenotype that is difficult to
    compare consistently across large numbers of samples. Its variation can
    reflect shared ancestry, convergence, and environmental selection. Image
    embeddings provide a scalable way to quantify this variation: a vision model
    converts each specimen photograph into a numerical representation. These
    embeddings place visually similar specimens near one another in trait space.
    This makes appearance measurable across hundreds of thousands of digitized
    specimens. I use these embeddings to identify visually similar species, map
    the morphospace of butterfly wing patterns, and test whether phenotypic
    similarity recovers known mimicry relationships. Combined with language
    models for natural-language search, this work shows how I use emerging AI
    tools to extract evolutionary information from natural history collections.
  </p>
  <EmbeddingPipeline />
</Content>

<SectionHead
  text="The butterfly morphospace: upper and under wings tell different stories"
/>
<Content>
  <p>
    Each point is a species centroid in a principal-component space of image
    embeddings of digitized butterfly specimens. Switch between the dorsal and
    ventral surfaces to see the shift in morphospace. Some families, such as the
    skippers (Hesperiidae) and whites and sulphurs (Pieridae) separate clearly,
    while many lineages change position between sides. Hover a point for its
    name, or pick a family to highlight it.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MorphospacePlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-4">
  {
    morphoStats.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
          {s.value}
        </dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
          {s.label}
        </dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<SectionHead text="Does visual similarity recover butterfly mimicry?" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Mimicry is a direct test of whether image embeddings capture what predators
    perceive. I compiled published Müllerian and Batesian butterfly mimicry
    pairs and, using dorsal wings, ranked how similar each
    partner is to the other among all species in the collection. Mimicry pairs
    rank far above chance, and above congeners. The misses are informative: in <i
      >Papilio polytes</i
    > and <i>P. glaucus</i> only some females mimic, and <i
      >Heliconius timareta</i
    > varies across its range. More work is needed to improve the accuracy of the
    methods. However, this initial work shows the potential of image embeddings to
    capture biologically meaningful visual similarity, and that this similarity can
    be used to study mimicry, species interactions, and phenotypic evolution. Use
    the interactive plots below to explore the results.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MimicryPlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
  {
    mimicryStats.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
          {s.value}
        </dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
          {s.label}
        </dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<SectionHead text="Choosing an open-weight LLM for natural-language search" />
<Content>
  <p>
    I designed natural-language search for a large specimen collection. The
    system uses a language model to parse a question such as “orange butterflies
    with black stripes from Peru” into structured search calls (color, country,
    trait, or similarity). To choose the best model for production, I benchmark
    open-weight models on the same set of queries, comparing accuracy,
    consistency, latency, and token cost. Open-weight models keep the search
    reproducible and let any institution with limited resources, run it on its
    own server.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <PlannerPlot client:visible />
</figure>

<SectionHead text="Putting it all together: BioCosmos" />
<Content>
  <p>
    <Link text="BioCosmos" url="https://lepiverse.com" /> brings this work together
    in a low-cost web platform, deployed through <Link
      text="LepiVerse.com"
      url="https://lepiverse.com/"
    />, for more than 600,000 digitized Lepidoptera specimen images. Image
    embeddings power visual-similarity search and interactive morphospace plots,
    an open-weight language model turns plain-language questions into searches,
    and taxonomic harmonization and geographic validation keep the underlying
    data clean. The result shows that emerging AI tools can make large natural
    history collections explorable for anyone with a browser.
  </p>
</Content>

<dl
  class="mx-auto mt-6 grid max-w-4xl grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-center sm:grid-cols-4"
>
  {
    collection.map((s) => (
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-4 dark:border-forest-100/10">
        <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
        <dd class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
          {s.value}
        </dd>
        <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
          {s.label}
        </dd>
      </div>
    ))
  }
</dl>

<div class="my-6 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-2">
  <a href="https://lepiverse.com" class={shotClasses}>
    <Image
      src={lepiverseHome}
      alt="LepiVerse home page with semantic, text, and image search beside a grid of butterfly specimens"
      width={900}
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-xl"
    />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100"
        >Semantic, text, and image search</span
      >
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400"
        >lepiverse.com</span
      >
    </p>
  </a>
  <a href="https://lepiverse.com/species/morpho_aega" class={shotClasses}>
    <Image
      src={lepiverseSpecies}
      alt="LepiVerse species page for Morpho aega with specimen images, classification, and distribution map"
      width={900}
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-xl"
    />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100"
        >Enriched species page</span
      >
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400"
        ><i>Morpho aega</i></span
      >
    </p>
  </a>
</div>

<figure class={shotClasses}>
  <Image
    src={lepiverseSimilar}
    alt="Visually similar species to Morpho aega. Dorsal matches are blue Morpho species; ventral matches include brown Morpho, Zeuxidia, Tanaecia, and Vindula species."
    width={1400}
    loading="lazy"
    class="w-full rounded-xl"
  />
  <figcaption class="px-2 pt-2 text-xs text-forest-800 dark:text-forest-200">
    <span class="font-semibold">Visually similar species.</span> Nearest neighbours
    of <i>Morpho aega</i> in embedding space. Dorsal matches are all blue
    <i>Morpho</i>, but the cryptic brown undersides also match <i>Zeuxidia</i>,
    <i>Tanaecia</i>, and <i>Vindula</i>, from different genera.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<SectionHead text="Future directions" />
<Content>
  <p>
    I hope to extend this framework to Indo-Australian rodents, asking whether
    phenotypic divergence among isolated island and mountain populations
    parallels genomic divergence or reflects local environments, and, through
    collaborations, to frogs, reptiles, and birds.
  </p>
</Content>
